{"step_index":52,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-19T19:14:05Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-20T00:44:05+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-20T00:44:10+05:30\nThe search for \"\"llama.cpp\" \"rpc-server\" OR \"rpc\" \"Mac Mini\" \"MacBook\"\" returned the following summary:\nTo use `llama.cpp` in a distributed setup across multiple Apple Silicon Macs (or a mix of Mac and PC), you can utilize its **RPC (Remote Procedure Call) backend**[1][2]. This allows you to split a large language model (LLM) across machines, effectively pooling their memory and GPU resources[1][3].\n\n### How the RPC Setup Works\n*   **Master Node:** The machine that runs your primary application (e.g., `llama-cli` or `llama-server`). It handles model metadata, tokenization, and orchestration[1][2].\n*   **Worker Node(s):** The machine(s) running the `rpc-server`. These machines contribute their RAM and GPU (Metal on Mac, CUDA on Nvidia) to process specific layers of the model[1][4].\n\n### Step-by-Step Setup\n1.  **Build with RPC Support:**\n    On both your primary (Master) and worker (Mac Mini/MacBook) machines, you must compile `llama.cpp` with RPC support[2][5].\n    ```bash\n    git clone https://github.com/ggerganov/llama.cpp\n    cd llama.cpp\n    cmake -B build -DGGML_METAL=ON -DGGML_RPC=ON\n    cmake --build build --config Release -j\n    ```\n    *Note: Use `-DGGML_METAL=ON` for Macs and `-DGGML_CUDA=ON` for Nvidia PCs[1][5].*\n\n2.  **Start the Worker (Mac Mini/MacBook):**\n    On the worker machine, launch the `rpc-server`[1][4]:\n    ```bash\n    ./build/bin/rpc-server -p 50052 -H 0.0.0.0\n    ```\n    *Ensure the machine's IP address is reachable from the master node and that the port (50052) is open in your firewall[4].*\n\n3.  **Run Inference from the Master:**\n    When running your model, use the `--rpc` flag to point to your worker node(s)[5]:\n    ```bash\n    ./build/bin/llama-cli -m your_model.gguf --rpc 192.168.1.50:50052 -ngl 99\n    ```\n    You can provide multiple comma-separated IP addresses if using more than one worker[5].\n\n### Important Considerations\n*   **Performance Bottleneck:** The primary limitation of this setup is network latency[6][3]. While local RAM is very fast, moving data between machines over standard Ethernet or Wi-Fi is significantly slower[7][2]. A 10GbE (10 Gigabit Ethernet) connection or a Thunderbolt bridge is highly recommended for better throughput[7][6].\n*   **Apple RDMA:** As of recent `llama.cpp` updates (v0.4.0+), if you are connecting two Macs via **Thunderbolt 5**, you can utilize RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access) for much higher speeds by bypassing the standard TCP network stack[8][9][10].\n*   **Alternative (Exo):** If you find the manual RPC setup too cumbersome, tools like **[exo](https://github.com/exo-explore/exo)** are designed specifically to \"auto-discover\" other Macs on your network and handle the partitioning of models with almost zero configuration[6][3].\n\nSources:\n[1] [theaiserver.in](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH_2KKjLfILP0MpXm8JGjlOVnZ3yxWtc_I9xLhCXm1fbq7gIWSnX1lJmLxBlTmr_-bDOrona_30pm7szBoeaBi4A1vwKT7IlWdi4EYh4gCKSzr1aMgCSP5e77huejsJ62-ztYTTym0DzhgK56WfBvEcUBg=)\n[2] [sharedllm.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFKaNLdIteiRJBgtoZuHsESTzjLEL_utOgaGZJEC7ZG4SdUaylxbi8B8UPglyt7d3x8mKPsTqNT04X6tD0wm_XvFxn8i7nFH7aqWU9s2VraF-STR6IsoQ9OCOrbNr5ia_YCxIvhjRMxF1ZS3g==)\n[3] [thinkdifferent.blog](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHCkta52QqlX5abosmsVBYb4zY02OLeAv0TFZrV3NGUMtrmyyYLOr_hyQB_uSnaw46hghXTZ5O2T_fRxU2dOUgZfdiCYsYArHVC2wr5Sktuas9U6Yby5AV5pdU98S-_QncNujD1HDqAH1EaRkbltzRn9EsDSlsfVNgIkt9SeZrZnezlsPcl2XumYiL_1jBu7arQ)\n[4] [theaiserver.in](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH_2KKjLfILP0MpXm8JGjlOVnZ3yxWtc_I9xLhCXm1fbq7gIWSnX1lJmLxBlTmr_-bDOrona_30pm7szBoeaBi4A1vwKT7IlWdi4EYh4gCKSzr1aMgCSP5e77huejsJ62