{"step_index":57,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-19T19:14:33Z","thinking":"Direct RAM expansion over Thunderbolt is fundamentally not feasible; the architecture doesn't support that type of external RAM access. Exploring the possibility of using the Mac Mini's RAM as a network-accessible RAM disk, the \"Share Disk\" function offers SMB sharing, which exclusively targets internal storage volumes.\n\n\n\nExporting a RAM disk over Thunderbolt IP results in significantly higher latency than internal memory or even local SSD swapping, rendering it impractical. The absence of OS-level solutions for transparent RAM pooling across Macs suggests that the architecture fundamentally prevents unified memory access due to hardware and OS design limitations. Application-level memory pooling or distributed computation frameworks appear to be more viable alternatives, with distributed AI inference representing a promising use case.\n\n\nInvestigating distributed computing approaches, such as model sharding across Macs using a specific implementation or RPC, seems more promising for leveraging multiple machines. Cluster computing frameworks like those for distributed AI or big data processing are worth examining, along with tools for distributed compilation and remote development.\n\nConsidering the limitations of the OS, attempts at network swap or remote swap space seem unlikely to work because core operating system features restrict memory configuration, and the historical mechanisms are deprecated.\n\n\nRDMA over Thunderbolt 5 offers very low latency and high bandwidth, but is limited to point-to-point connections, does not pool OS RAM, and requires specific hardware and setup. Ethernet RoCE/iWARP lacks macOS driver support.\n\nKernel extensions for memory sharing are highly restricted by hardware security features, rendering a functional, performant solution via this method extremely unlikely due to limitations in virtualizing or remapping memory.\n\n\nContainer orchestration tools like those for a specific platform do not provide RAM pooling across hosts; each container's memory is limited to the physical machine's capacity. Distributed hypervisors are unavailable, while the existing hypervisor framework lacks multi-host memory support.\n\nOlder cluster computing tools and message-passing libraries are functional but operate within isolated memory spaces and do not provide a unified RAM pool. Modern distributed AI frameworks present a more promising direction.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"# Research Report: Unconventional, Third-Party, and Hacker Methods for Combining Mac Mini & MacBook Pro RAM and Compute\n\n**Research Sub-Query:** Unconventional, Third-Party, and Hacker Methods  \n**Target Configuration:** Connecting an Apple Silicon Mac Mini and MacBook Pro to share/pool RAM and combine processing power.\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\n1. **Hardware/OS-Level RAM Expansion is Physically & Architecturally Impossible:** You cannot transparently expand a MacBook Pro's RAM using a Mac Mini over Thunderbolt or Ethernet. Unified Memory on Apple Silicon operates at 100–800 GB/s with 50–90 nanosecond latency directly on the SoC package. Thunderbolt 3/4 (PCIe tunneling at ~3.2 GB/s, 150–300 µs latency) and Thunderbolt 5 (~8–10 GB/s) are orders of magnitude too slow and high-latency for direct CPU cache/memory controller interaction. Furthermore, Apple Silicon does not support CXL (Compute Express Link), and modern macOS does not support Single System Image (SSI) memory virtualization.\n2. **Exported RAM Disks and Remote Network Swap Are Slower Than Internal SSD Swap:** While a Mac Mini can create a RAM disk and export it over Thunderbolt via iSCSI or Network Block Device (NBD), using it as remote virtual memory/swap on the MacBook produces worse performance than the MacBook’s own internal NVMe SSD (20–50 µs latency locally vs. 150–300 µs over Thunderbolt IP) and causes catastrophic kernel panics if packets drop.\n3. **The Breakthrough Hacker Reality — RDMA over Thunderbolt 5 (Apple Technote TN3205):** Starting in macOS 26.2, Apple introduced native **RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access) over Thunderbolt 5** for Apple Silicon Macs equipped with Thunderbolt 5 ports (e.g., M4 Pro / M4 Max Mac mini and MacBook Pro). Enabled in macOS Recovery via `rdma_ctl enable`, this bypasses the standard TCP/IP network stack, dropping interconnect latency from ~300 µs to ~3–5 µs at 50–80 Gbps. It does not merge OS-level RAM for general applications, but it enables **shared memory pooling for distributed AI inference and tensor parallelism**.\n4. **Application-Level Resource Pooling is the Functional Path:** Modern frameworks—principally **Exo**, **Apple MLX Distributed (JACCL backend)**, and **llama.cpp RPC (`rpc-server`)**—effectively pool the unified memory and Metal GPU processing power of both Macs to run frontier AI models (e.g., DeepSeek, Llama 3.3, Command R+) that neither machine could run alone. For general computing, frameworks like **Ray**, **OpenMPI**, **distcc**, and distributed render engines (Blender, DaVinci, Compressor) distribute processing without shared OS memory.\n\n---\n\n## Source Quality Classification\n\n* **Tier A (Official Documentation, Vendor Technotes, Primary System Frameworks):**\n  * Apple Technote TN3205: *Low-latency communication with RDMA over Thunderbolt* [apple.com]\n  * Apple Developer Documentation: *DriverKit, System Extensions, and Mach VM Subsystem* [apple.com]\n  * Apple Historical Documentation: *Xgrid Architecture and NetBoot Configuration Guidelines* [apple.com]\n* **Tier B (Reputable Engineering Analyses, Academic Papers, Hardware Benchmarks):**\n  * Jeff Geerling: *1.5 TB of VRAM on Mac Studio – RDMA over Thunderbolt 5* (Dec 2025) [jeffgeerling.com]\n  * Amza et al. (Rice University / USENIX): *TreadMarks: Shared Memory Computing on Networks of Workstations* [usenix.org]\n  * GiantVM Consortium / IEEE: *GiantVM: A Distributed Hypervisor for Many-to-One Virtualization* [researchgate.net]\n  * CXL Consortium Specifications: *Compute Express Link Architecture and Disaggregated Memory* [computeexpresslink.org]\n* **Tier C (Developer Forums, Open-Source Repositories, Community Discussions):**\n  * GitHub: `exo-explore/exo` — Distributed LLM cluster orchestration for Apple Silicon\n  * GitHub: `ggerganov/llama.cpp` — GGML RPC backend architecture (`-DGGML_RPC=ON`)\n  * GitHub: `ml-explore/mlx` & `alexziskind1/mlx-jaccl-cluster` — JACCL collective communication over RDMA\n  * StackExchange / Reddit r/macsysadmin: In-depth dynamic_pager dissection and SIP enforcement\n\n---\n\n## 1. External RAM Expansion / RAM Disk over Thunderbolt\n\n### Mechanics & Feasibility\n* **Direct Memory Bus Extension:** Impossible. RAM operates synchronously on a parallel memory bus (or tightly integrated LPDDR5/LPDDR5X on-package Unified Memory) clocked in gigahertz with latencies under 100 ns. Thunderbolt is a serial packetized PCIe/DisplayPort tunneling protocol. A processor cannot issue load/store instructions (`LDR`/`STR`) directly across Thunderbolt.\n* **Apple \"Target Disk Mode\" / \"Share Disk\":**\n  * On Intel Macs, Target Disk Mode exposed the physical ATA/SATA/NVMe controller over FireWire or Thunderbolt.\n  * On Apple Silicon Macs, Target Disk Mode was replaced by **\"Share Disk\"**, which boots into a minimal Recovery Mode SMB server.\n  * *Limitation:* It strictly shares mounted APFS partitions on internal physical NAND flash. It does not and cannot advertise volatile RAM.\n* **Network-Exported RAM Disk (The Hacker Setup):**\n  * A user can allocate physical memory on the Mac Mini as a virtual ramdisk:\n    ```bash\n    # Allocate a 16GB RAM disk on Mac Mini\n    diskutil erasevolume HFS+ \"RAMDisk\" $(hdid -nomount ram://33554432)\n    ```\n  * To expose it to the MacBook Pro as a raw block device, one must configure an **iSCSI Target** (via third-party software like KernSafe iStorage Server or a Linux VM running `tgt`/LIO) or **NBD (Network Block Device)** running over a direct point-to-point IP-over-Thunderbolt Bridge (20–32 Gbps on TB3/TB4, ~64 Gbps on TB5).\n  * The MacBook Pro connects as an initiator and mounts the block device.\n\n### Latency, Bandwidth & Memory Hierarchy Comparison\n\n| Medium | Sequential Bandwidth | Random Latency | Access Paradigm |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Apple Silicon Unified Memory (M4/M4 Pro/Max)** | 150 – 546 GB/s | 50 – 90 ns | Direct hardware memory controller bus |\n| **Internal Apple NVMe SSD** | 3.5 – 7.2 GB/s | 20 – 50 µs | PCIe NVMe Controller (local) |\n| **Thunderbolt 5 RDMA (Point-to-Point)** | 8.0 – 10.0 GB/s | 3 – 5 µs | User-space RDMA transport |\n| **Thunderbolt 3/4 IP-over-TB RAM Disk (iSCSI/NBD)**| 2.5 – 3.5 GB/s | 150 – 300 µs | Kernel TCP/IP stack + block SCSI emulation |\n| **10GbE Network Share (NFS/SMB)** | 1.1 GB/s | 500 – 1,000 µs | Network filesystem layer |\n\n### Counter-Evidence & Reality Check\nEven though the Mac Mini's RAM is intrinsically faster than flash memory, wrapping that RAM in an IP-over-Thunderbolt protocol stack introduces a **150–300 microsecond latency penalty** (roughly 3,000 times slower than physical RAM). Local NVMe swap on the MacBook Pro's internal SSD responds in **20–50 microseconds** (5x to 10x faster than the remote RAM disk). Exporting a RAM disk across Thunderbolt to expand memory is objectively inferior to local macOS SSD paging.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Distributed Shared Memory (DSM) & Third-Party RAM Pooling Hacks\n\n### Why OS-Level RAM Pooling Does Not Exist for macOS\n* **Cache Coherence Barrier:** Modern multi-core systems require hardware cache coherency (e.g., MESI/MOESI protocols) implemented in hardware snooping logic and on-chip interconnects. Separate Macs do not have a hardware cache-coherent bus.\n* **The Software DSM Precedents (TreadMarks, Kerrighed, OpenSSI):**\n  * In the 1990s and 2000s, academic software DSMs like **TreadMarks** (Rice University) implemented Lazy Release Consistency (LRC) using virtual memory page protections (`mprotect`) to trap access violations (`SIGSEGV`), intercept read/write faults, and fetch pages over Ethernet.\n  * *Failure Mode:* The \"ping-pong effect\" (false sharing). If two Macs write to independent variables situated in the same 4KB or 16KB memory page, the page bounces repeatedly across the network, leading to complete bus saturation and computational collapse.\n\n### Application-Level Distributed In-Memory Grids\nWhile general macOS applications cannot pool RAM, developers and hackers achieve distributed memory pooling at the application layer:\n1. **Ray Plasma Object Store / Apache Arrow:**\n   * A shared-memory object store distributed across cluster nodes. Nodes pool memory to hold serialized datasets, training weights, and tabular data accessible via IPC with zero-copy deserialization.\n2. **In-Memory Distributed Datastores (Redis Cluster / Memcached / Hazelcast):**\n   * Used to aggregate tens of gigabytes of RAM across machines for rapid read/write key-value caching.\n3. **MemCloud:**\n   * Peer-to-peer local network memory aggregation daemon allowing machines to contribute unallocated RAM to a shared distributed cache.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) over Thunderbolt & Ethernet\n\n### Apple Technote TN3205: RDMA over Thunderbolt 5 (macOS 26.2+)\nIn late 2025/2026, Apple introduced native support for point-to-point **RDMA over Thunderbolt 5**:\n* **Hardware Requirements:** Apple Silicon Macs with **Thunderbolt 5 controllers** (e.g., M4 Pro Mac mini, M4 Pro/Max MacBook Pro).\n  * *Evidence/Quote (Technote TN3205):* RDMA transport drivers are tied to Apple’s PCIe Gen 4/5 Thunderbolt controllers; Thunderbolt 3 and 4 controllers lack the hardware queue pair and DMA engine support, falling back to standard TCP/IP networking.\n* **Activation Process:**\n  1. Boot Mac into macOS Recovery (hold Power button at startup).\n  2. Launch Terminal from Utilities and run:\n     ```bash\n     rdma_ctl enable\n     ```\n  3. Reboot into normal macOS.\n  4. Verify interfaces using the OpenFabrics Alliance verbs utility:\n     ```bash\n     ibv_devices\n     # Lists: rdma_en2, rdma_en3, etc.\n     ```\n* **Performance:** Reduces interconnect latency from **~300 µs (standard TCP/IP)** down to **3–5 µs**, unlocking 50–80 Gbps bi-directional throughput directly between memory buffers.\n* **Cluster Topologies:** No commercial Thunderbolt 5 network switches exist; clusters must use direct **full-mesh point-to-point cabling** (e.g., direct dual-cables between Mac mini and MacBook Pro, or 4 nodes cross-connected).\n* **Scope Limitation:** This is **not** an OS-level RAM merger. macOS Finder, Safari, and standard apps do not see or use this memory. It exposes an InfiniBand/verbs interface accessible exclusively to RDMA-aware software.\n\n### RDMA over Ethernet (RoCE / iWARP) on macOS\n* There is **no native RoCE or iWARP support** in macOS for third-party PCIe Ethernet NICs (such as Mellanox ConnectX or Chelsio cards).\n* While Chelsio provides macOS drivers for 10G/25G/100G adapters, they function strictly as Layer-2 NICs; the RDMA offload engine is disabled due to missing kernel verbs architecture in standard macOS.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Network Swap and Remote Virtual Memory\n\n### Historical NetBoot vs. Modern macOS XNU Architecture\n* **Historical Precedent:** Mac OS X (10.4 Tiger through 10.13 High Sierra) supported **NetBoot**, where diskless clients mounted root or shadow filesystems over NFS or AFP. Under NetBoot, `/private/var/vm` could reside on an NFS mount managed by `dynamic_pager` and `nbdst` (NetBoot Deferred Shadow Tool).\n* **Modern macOS Reality (macOS 11 Big Sur through macOS 15/26):**\n  * Apple completely deprecated and removed NetBoot.\n  * **System Integrity Protection (SIP)** and the **Signed System Volume (SSV)** seal the filesystem. The `dynamic_pager` user-space daemon was retired; page file creation and swap compression (`vm_compressor`) were absorbed directly into the XNU kernel Mach VM core (`vm_swap.c`).\n  * Modern macOS strictly mandates that swapfiles (`/System/Volumes/VM/swapfile*`) reside exclusively on the encrypted, authenticated APFS container on local solid-state storage.\n* **Hacker Experimentation & Failure Modes:**\n  * Attempting to symlink `/System/Volumes/VM` or mount an NFS/SMB share over the swap directory requires disabling SIP (`csrutil disable`), breaking SSV verification (`csrutil authenticated-root disable`), and modifying Mach VM kernel boot parameters.\n  * *Catastrophic Failure:* Virtual memory demands deterministic, non-blocking I/O. If a single network packet is dropped, a latency spike occurs, or the Thunderbolt cable is unplugged during a page fault, the kernel encounters an unhandled page fault exception in kernel mode, triggering an instantaneous **Kernel Panic (Panic Caller: Mach VM)**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Kernel Extensions, DriverKit, and Low-Level Jailbreaks\n\n### Architectural Constraints on Apple Silicon\n* **Kext Deprecation & Reduced Security:** Traditional Kernel Extensions (`.kext`) running in Ring 0 are heavily deprecated. To load any third-party kext on Apple Silicon, the user must enter Recovery Mode and lower the system security policy from \"Full Security\" to \"Reduced Security\".\n* **Hardware Page Protection Mechanisms:**\n  * **KTRR (Kernel Text Read-only Region):** Prevents modification of running kernel code even with root/kernel privileges.\n  * **APRR (Access Protection and Routing Registers):** Enforces strict memory page permission boundaries between user-space and kernel memory.\n* **DriverKit Restrictions:** Modern macOS drivers run in user space via **DriverKit** (`.dext`). DriverKit runs sandboxed with restricted entitlements. It has zero APIs to intercept the Mach VM subsystem or remap physical memory addresses across a peripheral bus.\n* **Verdict on Memory-Sharing Kexts:** No functioning, maintained kernel extension or jailbreak exists that transparently expands macOS RAM across network links. The complexity of handling cache coherence, page table synchronization, and non-blocking page-ins without crashing the kernel makes software-only kernel DSM practically unviable.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Distributed AI Inference & Memory Pooling (The Proven Working Solution)\n\nWhile the operating system cannot pool RAM for general-purpose computing, the open-source and hacker communities have solved the memory-pooling problem for the most memory-intensive workload in modern computing: **Large Language Model (LLM) Inference and Training**.\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                  USER CLIENT                                  |\n|         (OpenAI-Compatible API / llama-cli / Python Prompt Script)            |\n+---------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+\n                                        |\n                                        v\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       PRIMARY NODE (MacBook Pro - Master)                     |\n|  - Manages Tokenization, Context Window & KV Cache                            |\n|  - Holds Layers 1 to 24 in Local Unified Memory (e.g., 18 GB used)            |\n|  - Executes Forward Passes on Local M-Series GPU (Metal)                      |\n+---------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+\n                                        |\n             Thunderbolt Bridge / RDMA (3-5 µs latency / 40-80 Gbps)\n                                        |\n                                        v\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        WORKER NODE (Mac Mini - Server)                        |\n|  - llama.cpp rpc-server / Exo Node / MLX JACCL Worker                         |\n|  - Holds Layers 25 to 48 in Local Unified Memory (e.g., 18 GB used)           |\n|  - Executes Forward Passes on Remote M-Series GPU (Metal)                     |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n### Framework 1: Exo (`exo-explore/exo`)\n* **How it Works:** Exo is an open-source peer-to-peer distributed inference engine built specifically for Apple Silicon. It auto-discovers other Macs on the local network (via Bonjour/mDNS) and shards model weights across available unified memory pools.\n* **Mechanism:** Uses **Pipeline Parallelism** and **Tensor Parallelism** with an optimized Apple MLX backend.\n* **Supported Interconnect:** Seamlessly operates over Wi-Fi, 10GbE, and Thunderbolt Bridge. On Thunderbolt 5 Macs running macOS 26.2+, it leverages the underlying RDMA layer for maximum throughput.\n* **Capability:** Allows a 16GB MacBook Pro and a 16GB Mac Mini to pool their collective 32GB of Unified Memory, enabling both machines to run a quantized 32B model (e.g., Qwen 2.5 32B or DeepSeek-Coder 33B) that neither could load individually.\n\n### Framework 2: Apple MLX Distributed with JACCL Backend\n* **Architecture:** MLX is Apple’s open-source array framework for Apple Silicon. The **JACCL (Jack and Angelos' Collective Communication Library)** backend is integrated directly into MLX for distributed tensor communication.\n* **Usage:** Configured via `mx.distributed.init(backend=\"jaccl\")` or launched across nodes:\n  ```bash\n  mlx.launch --backend jaccl --hosts 192.168.10.1,192.168.10.2 python distributed_script.py\n  ```\n* **Performance:** When paired with Thunderbolt 5 point-to-point connections and `rdma_ctl enable`, JACCL achieves sub-10 µs tensor synchronization, delivering near-linear scaling across interconnected Mac hardware.\n\n### Framework 3: llama.cpp Remote Procedure Call (RPC)\n* **Architecture:** `llama.cpp` features a native RPC backend (`-DGGML_RPC=ON`) that treats the Mac Mini as a remote Metal accelerator.\n* **Setup Workflow:**\n  1. *On Mac Mini (Worker):*\n     ```bash\n     cmake -B build -DGGML_METAL=ON -DGGML_RPC=ON\n     cmake --build build --config Release -j\n     ./build/bin/rpc-server -p 50052 -H 192.168.10.2\n     ```\n  2. *On MacBook Pro (Master):*\n     ```bash\n     ./build/bin/llama-cli -m model.gguf --rpc 192.168.10.2:50052 -ngl 99\n     ```\n* **Result:** The model weights are automatically split across both machines' RAM, and tensor computations execute across both M-series GPUs in parallel.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Container, Hypervisor, and Cluster Computing Methods\n\n### Docker Swarm, Kubernetes, and Nomad\n* **Mechanism:** Orchestrators group independent machines into a logical cluster to schedule discrete containers (Pods or Tasks).\n* **Memory Limitation:** Container orchestrators **cannot aggregate RAM across nodes for a single container**. If a container requires 32GB of RAM, it cannot be scheduled on a cluster of two 16GB Macs; it will remain in an unschedulable state.\n* **Practical Utility:** Enables distributed microservices architectures, running batch workers on the Mac Mini while development runs on the MacBook Pro.\n\n### Distributed Hypervisors & Single System Image (SSI) VMs\n* **Academic/Enterprise Solutions:** Systems like **GiantVM** (distributed QEMU-KVM hypervisor) and **ScaleMP / TidalScale** virtualize multiple physical nodes into one massive multi-socket virtual machine running a single guest OS.\n* **macOS Incompatibility:**\n  * Apple’s native virtualization framework (`Hypervisor.framework` / `Virtualization.framework`) does not support distributed memory mapping.\n  * GiantVM and ScaleMP require modified bare-metal Linux kernels and custom hypervisors on x86/ARM server hardware; they cannot run macOS guests natively on Apple Silicon.\n\n### Distributed Compilation & Processing Frameworks\n1. **Distributed C/C++/Rust Compilation (`distcc`, `xmake`, `sccache`):**\n   * Preprocesses code locally on the MacBook Pro, transmits ASTs or compilation units over a Thunderbolt IP link to the Mac Mini, and compiles concurrently across all CPU cores.\n2. **Distributed Media Rendering:**\n   * **Blender Network Render:** Chunks animation frames and distributes them across both Macs.\n   * **Apple Compressor / DaVinci Resolve Studio Remote Rendering:** Uses the Mac Mini as a dedicated background rendering node over high-speed networking while editing continues on the MacBook Pro.\n3. **High-Performance Cluster Computing (OpenMPI / Slurm / Ray):**\n   * Standard MPI implementations (`brew install open-mpi`) run natively on Apple Silicon. Applications explicitly partition data and exchange memory buffers via non-blocking message passing (`MPI_Isend` / `MPI_Irecv`).\n\n---\n\n## Comprehensive Summary: Feasibility Matrix\n\n| Method | Feasibility | Pools RAM? | Combines Processing? | Bottleneck / Primary Risk |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Direct Thunderbolt RAM Expansion** | **Impossible** | No | No | Fundamental physics; lack of CXL / memory bus access. |\n| **iSCSI / NBD RAM Disk over Thunderbolt** | **Functional Hack** | No (Acts as Disk) | No | 150–300 µs latency (worse than local SSD swap). |\n| **Remote Network Swap (NFS/APFS)** | **Disabled / Blocked** | No | No | Instant Kernel Panic on packet drop or latency spike. |\n| **Kernel Extensions / Jailbreak for DSM** | **Impractical / Unviable** | No | No | KTRR/APRR protections, lack of hardware cache coherency. |\n| **RDMA over Thunderbolt 5 (`rdma_ctl`)** | **Officially Supported** | Yes (via Verbs/API) | Yes (Distributed ML) | Restricted to TB5 hardware (M4 Pro/Max) and RDMA-aware apps. |\n| **Exo / llama.cpp RPC / MLX JACCL** | **Fully Functional** | **Yes (Model VRAM)** | **Yes (Metal GPU)** | High network traffic; restricted to AI/LLM workloads. |\n| **Docker Swarm / Kubernetes** | **Fully Functional** | No (Per-pod only) | Yes (Containerized) | Single containers cannot exceed physical host RAM. |\n| **Distributed Compile / Render (`distcc`, Ray)**| **Fully Functional** | No (Task-level) | Yes (CPU/GPU Tasks) | Application must explicitly support distributed scheduling. |\n\n---\n\n## Final Recommendation for the User\n\n1. **If the goal is to run Large AI/LLM models beyond the RAM of either machine:**\n   * Connect the Mac Mini and MacBook Pro directly with a high-bandwidth Thunderbolt cable (enabling a Thunderbolt Bridge IP connection).\n   * If both devices feature **Thunderbolt 5** (M4 Pro / M4 Max) and run macOS 26.2+, enable RDMA via Recovery Mode (`rdma_ctl enable`) to unlock 3–5 µs latency.\n   * Deploy **Exo** (`brew install exo`) or compile **`llama.cpp` with RPC support** (`-DGGML_RPC=ON`). This merges the Unified Memory and GPU cores of both machines into an aggregated AI inference cluster.\n2. **If the goal is general computing, software development, or rendering:**\n   * Treat the Mac Mini as a high-performance headless compute server.\n   * Use **VS Code Remote Tunnels / SSH**, **distcc/sccache** for distributed compilation, or network render nodes in **Blender/Compressor**.\n   * Avoid any attempts to redirect macOS virtual memory or swapfiles over the network, as it degrades performance compared to local SSD swap and introduces severe system instability.","Recipient":"1b146f06-0d46-4922-b656-a59fd584eb61","toolAction":"Sending report to parent","toolSummary":"Send complete research findings to parent"}}]}
